  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Ohio received $220M in federal contract obligations in FY2027 to date. Obligations fell 97.4% year over year. Contracting produced 912 awards with an average award size of $241K. The state saw much larger totals in prior years: FY22 totaled $7.8B, FY23 totaled $10.3B, FY24 totaled $9.4B, FY25 totaled $10B, and FY26 totaled $8.3B. The Department of Veterans Affairs led buying in FY2027 to date with $96.3M. The Department of Energy followed with $63.5M. The General Services Administration obligated $17.6M and the Department of Health and Human Services obligated $13M. The largest vendor by obligations in FY2027 to date is SOUTHERN OHIO CLEANUP COMPANY LLC with $63.3M. The Remediation Services industry accounted for $63.8M and Commercial and Institutional Building Construction accounted for $44.2M. All figures are contract obligations; current-year totals are partial (fiscal year to date).",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "What explains the sharp year-over-year decline in FY2027 obligations?",
        "body": "Obligations fell 97.4% year over year. Prior fiscal years show substantially higher totals, including $10.3B in FY23 and $10B in FY25. The Department of Veterans Affairs accounted for $96.3M of FY2027 obligations and the Department of Energy accounted for $63.5M, indicating a concentration of current-year spending among a few agencies."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Which agencies, vendors, and industries dominate FY2027 to date?",
        "body": "The Department of Veterans Affairs is the top buyer with $96.3M and the Department of Energy is second with $63.5M. SOUTHERN OHIO CLEANUP COMPANY LLC is the top vendor with $63.3M and the Remediation Services industry leads with $63.8M. Commercial and Institutional Building Construction is the second-largest industry with $44.2M."
      }
    ]
  },
